13: # Determinism, Parity & Common Recipes
14: 
15: Copy-paste boundary templates, naming conventions, deterministic recipes, and Cargo commands for Rust development in OpenCode.
16: 
17: ---
18: 
19: ## 1. OVERVIEW
20: 
21: ### Purpose
22: 
23: Provides the recipe-level numeric, sort, identifier and serialization recipes and the replay commands that make a Rust result match the pinned TypeScript bytes.
24: 
25: ### When to Use
26: 
27: - Converting a TypeScript rounding, `toFixed` or fixed-six expression
28: - Building a stable sort, a deterministic ID or a canonical map or serialization
29: - Adding a golden replay or determinism replay across boundaries
30: - Testing a forced WASI fallback path
31: 
32: ---
33: 
34: ## 2. DETERMINISM, PARITY, AND COMMON RECIPES
35: 
36: ### Finite Score and Signed-Zero Policy
37: 
38: ```rust
39: use thiserror::Error;
40: 
41: #[derive(Debug, Error)]
42: enum ScoreError {
43:     #[error("score must be finite")]
44:     NonFinite,
45: }
46: 
47: fn normalize_score(value: f64) -> Result<f64, ScoreError> {
48:     if !value.is_finite() {
49:         return Err(ScoreError::NonFinite);
50:     }
51: 
52:     // This boundary contract serializes both signs of zero as positive zero.
53:     Ok(if value == 0.0 { 0.0 } else { value })
54: }
55: ```
56: 
57: Do not normalize `-0.0` unless the TypeScript oracle does. Test `value.to_bits()` when signed zero is contractually observable.
58: 
59: ### TypeScript `Math.round(value * 1e6) / 1e6`
60: 
61: ```rust
62: const SCALE: f64 = 1_000_000.0;
63: 
64: fn quantize_like_math_round_six(value: f64) -> Result<f64, ScoreError> {
65:     if !value.is_finite() {
66:         return Err(ScoreError::NonFinite);
67:     }
68: 
69:     let scaled = value * SCALE;
70:     if !scaled.is_finite() {
71:         return Err(ScoreError::NonFinite);
72:     }
73: 
74:     let floor = scaled.floor();
75:     let fraction = scaled - floor;
76:     let rounded = if fraction < 0.5 { floor } else { floor + 1.0 };
77: 
78:     let rounded = if rounded == 0.0 && scaled.is_sign_negative() {
79:         -0.0
80:     } else {
81:         rounded
82:     };
83: 
84:     Ok(rounded / SCALE)
85: }
86: ```
87: 
88: JavaScript `Math.round` resolves exact halfway cases toward positive infinity and can return negative zero. Rust `f64::round` resolves halfway cases away from zero and is not a compatible substitute.
89: 
90: ### TypeScript `Number(value.toFixed(6))`
91: 
92: `toFixed(6)` is a decimal conversion contract, not binary `f64::round`. Keep it separate from the `Math.round` helper. The following bounded implementation uses the exact binary value and decimal integer arithmetic for finite values whose absolute magnitude is below `1e21`:
93: 
94: ```rust
95: use num_bigint::BigUint;
96: use num_traits::{One, Zero};
97: use thiserror::Error;
98: 
99: const DECIMAL_PLACES: usize = 6;
100: const DECIMAL_SCALE: u64 = 1_000_000;
101: 
102: #[derive(Debug, Error)]
103: enum FixedError {
104:     #[error("value must be finite")]
105:     NonFinite,
106: 
107:     #[error("absolute value must be below 1e21")]
108:     OutOfContractRange,
109: 
110:     #[error("generated decimal could not be parsed as f64")]
111:     Parse,
112: }
113: 
114: fn quantize_like_number_to_fixed_six(value: f64) -> Result<f64, FixedError> {
115:     let text = to_fixed_six_text(value)?;
116:     text.parse::<f64>().map_err(|_| FixedError::Parse)
117: }
118: 
119: fn to_fixed_six_text(value: f64) -> Result<String, FixedError> {
120:     if !value.is_finite() {
121:         return Err(FixedError::NonFinite);
122:     }
123:     if value.abs() >= 1e21 {
124:         return Err(FixedError::OutOfContractRange);
125:     }
126: 
127:     let negative = value < 0.0;
128:     let rounded = rounded_scaled_integer(value.abs(), DECIMAL_SCALE);
129:     let mut digits = rounded.to_str_radix(10);
130: 
131:     if digits.len() <= DECIMAL_PLACES {
132:         let zeros = "0".repeat(DECIMAL_PLACES + 1 - digits.len());
133:         digits = format!("{zeros}{digits}");
134:     }
135: 
136:     let split = digits.len() - DECIMAL_PLACES;
137:     let sign = if negative { "-" } else { "" };
138:     Ok(format!("{sign}{}.{}", &digits[..split], &digits[split..]))
139: }
140: 
141: fn rounded_scaled_integer(value: f64, scale: u64) -> BigUint {
142:     if value == 0.0 {
143:         return BigUint::zero();
144:     }
145: 
146:     let bits = value.to_bits();
147:     let exponent_bits = ((bits >> 52) & 0x7ff) as i32;
148:     let fraction_bits = bits & ((1_u64 << 52) - 1);
149: 
150:     let (significand, binary_exponent) = if exponent_bits == 0 {
151:         (BigUint::from(fraction_bits), -1_074)
152:     } else {
153:         (
154:             BigUint::from((1_u64 << 52) | fraction_bits),
155:             exponent_bits - 1_023 - 52,
156:         )
157:     };
158: 
159:     let numerator = significand * BigUint::from(scale);
160:     if binary_exponent >= 0 {
161:         return numerator << usize::try_from(binary_exponent).unwrap_or_default();
162:     }
163: 
164:     let shift = usize::try_from(-binary_exponent).unwrap_or_default();
165:     let denominator = BigUint::one() << shift;
166:     let quotient = &numerator / &denominator;
167:     let remainder = numerator % &denominator;
168: 
169:     if (&remainder << 1_usize) >= denominator {
170:         quotient + BigUint::one()
171:     } else {
172:         quotient
173:     }
174: }
175: ```
176: 
177: The bounded range is deliberate. ECMAScript switches to ordinary number-to-string behavior at `1e21`; implement and golden-test that separate path only if the boundary contract permits values in that range. Do not replace this helper with `format!("{value:.6}")`, `f64::to_string`, `ryu`, or `serde_json`.
178: 
179: ### Fixed-Six Text
180: 
181: ```rust
182: fn fixed_six_text(value: f64) -> Result<String, FixedError> {
183:     to_fixed_six_text(value)
184: }
185: ```
186: 
187: Keep fixed-six text separate from numeric quantization. `"1.230000"` and the number `1.23` are not byte-equivalent wire values.
188: 
189: ### Stable Sort With Terminal Tie-Break
190: 
191: ```rust
192: use std::cmp::Ordering;
193: 
194: #[derive(Debug)]
195: struct Candidate {
196:     score: f64,
197:     source_priority: u32,
198:     id: String,
199: }
200: 
201: fn compare_candidates(left: &Candidate, right: &Candidate) -> Ordering {
202:     right
203:         .score
204:         .total_cmp(&left.score)
205:         .then_with(|| left.source_priority.cmp(&right.source_priority))
206:         .then_with(|| left.id.cmp(&right.id))
207: }
208: 
209: fn sort_candidates(candidates: &mut [Candidate]) {
210:     candidates.sort_by(compare_candidates);
211: }
212: ```
213: 
214: Validate scores as finite before sorting. Verify that Rust string ordering matches the permitted TypeScript identifier alphabet. Do not assume equivalence with `localeCompare`.
215: 
216: Never use `sort_unstable*` for parity-visible output, stable input order as the final tie-break, or `partial_cmp(...).unwrap()`.
217: 
218: ### Exact Deterministic-ID Construction
219: 
220: ```rust
221: use sha2::{Digest, Sha256};
222: 
223: fn deterministic_id(namespace: &str, source_id: &str, text: &str) -> String {
224:     let mut hasher = Sha256::new();
225: 
226:     // These fields, separators, UTF-8 bytes, and their order define the ID contract.
227:     hasher.update(namespace.as_bytes());
228:     hasher.update(b"\0");
229:     hasher.update(source_id.as_bytes());
230:     hasher.update(b"\0");
231:     hasher.update(text.as_bytes());
232: 
233:     let digest = hasher.finalize();
234:     hex::encode(digest).chars().take(24).collect()
235: }
236: ```
237: 
238: The TypeScript oracle defines:
239: 
240: - Included fields
241: - Field order
242: - Delimiters and escaping
243: - UTF-8 encoding
244: - Hash algorithm
245: - Lowercase or uppercase encoding
246: - Truncation unit and length
247: 
248: Do not improve or redesign the preimage inside a parity migration.
249: 
250: ### BTreeMap Canonicalization
251: 
252: ```rust
253: use std::collections::BTreeMap;
254: 
255: fn canonical_object(
256:     entries: impl IntoIterator<Item = (String, String)>,
257: ) -> BTreeMap<String, String> {
258:     entries.into_iter().collect()
259: }
260: ```
261: 
262: ### Collect-and-Sort Canonicalization
263: 
264: ```rust
265: fn canonical_edges(
266:     edges: impl IntoIterator<Item = (String, String)>,
267: ) -> Vec<(String, String)> {
268:     let mut edges: Vec<_> = edges.into_iter().collect();
269:     edges.sort_by(|left, right| {
270:         left.0
271:             .cmp(&right.0)
272:             .then_with(|| left.1.cmp(&right.1))
273:     });
274:     edges.dedup();
275:     edges
276: }
277: ```
278: 
279: ### Canonical Byte Serialization
280: 
281: ```rust
282: use serde::Serialize;
283: use thiserror::Error;
284: 
285: #[derive(Debug, Error)]
286: enum SerializationError {
287:     #[error("canonical JSON serialization failed: {0}")]
288:     Json(#[from] serde_json::Error),
289: }
290: 
291: fn canonical_json_line<T: Serialize>(
292:     value: &T,
293: ) -> Result<Vec<u8>, SerializationError> {
294:     let mut bytes = serde_json::to_vec(value)?;
295:     bytes.push(b'\n');
296:     Ok(bytes)
297: }
298: ```
299: 
300: Use structs for fixed field order and `BTreeMap` for dynamic object keys. `serde_json` is acceptable only when golden fixtures prove that field omission, escaping, numeric spelling, and newline behavior match the TypeScript serializer. Semantic JSON comparison is diagnostic only.
301: 
302: ### Narrow Unsafe Wrapper
303: 
304: ```rust
305: #![deny(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn)]
306: 
307: use thiserror::Error;
308: 
309: #[derive(Debug, Error, PartialEq, Eq)]
310: enum CopyError {
311:     #[error("requested prefix exceeds the source length")]
312:     LengthOutOfRange,
313: }
314: 
315: fn copy_prefix(bytes: &[u8], length: usize) -> Result<Vec<u8>, CopyError> {
316:     if length > bytes.len() {
317:         return Err(CopyError::LengthOutOfRange);
318:     }
319: 
320:     // SAFETY: bytes.as_ptr() is valid and aligned for u8 reads, length was
321:     // checked against the live slice, the source remains borrowed for the
322:     // operation, and the returned Vec owns a copy rather than an alias.
323:     let prefix = unsafe { std::slice::from_raw_parts(bytes.as_ptr(), length) };
324:     Ok(prefix.to_vec())
325: }
326: 
327: #[cfg(test)]
328: mod tests {
329:     use super::{copy_prefix, CopyError};
330: 
331:     #[test]
332:     fn copies_a_valid_prefix() -> Result<(), CopyError> {
333:         assert_eq!(copy_prefix(b"abcdef", 3)?, b"abc");
334:         Ok(())
335:     }
336: 
337:     #[test]
338:     fn rejects_a_length_beyond_the_live_slice() {
339:         assert_eq!(
340:             copy_prefix(b"abc", 4),
341:             Err(CopyError::LengthOutOfRange),
342:         );
343:     }
344: 
345:     #[test]
346:     fn accepts_an_empty_prefix() -> Result<(), CopyError> {
347:         assert!(copy_prefix(b"", 0)?.is_empty());
348:         Ok(())
349:     }
350: }
351: ```
352: 
353: The pure core uses `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]`; place reviewed unsafe wrappers in the narrow boundary crate. Every unsafe block needs an adjacent invariant, an exercising test, and a precondition-challenge test.
354: 
355: ### Golden Replay Across Boundaries
356: 
357: ```bash
358: # Generate expected bytes only from the pinned TypeScript oracle
359: node test/oracle/generate-goldens.mjs \
360:   --inputs test/goldens/inputs \
361:   --output /tmp/typescript-goldens
362: 
363: # Require committed expected bytes to match the oracle exactly
364: diff -ru test/goldens/expected /tmp/typescript-goldens
365: 
366: # Replay the same corpus through every shipped Rust boundary
367: cargo test -p ranking-core --test parity_golden --locked
368: cargo test -p ranking-napi --test boundary_native --locked
369: cargo test -p ranking-wasm --test boundary_wasm --locked
370: 
371: # Compare output as raw bytes without JSON normalization
372: cmp test/goldens/expected/results.jsonl \
373:   target/parity/native/results.jsonl
374: cmp test/goldens/expected/results.jsonl \
375:   target/parity/wasm/results.jsonl
376: cmp test/goldens/expected/results.jsonl \
377:   target/parity/wasi/results.jsonl
378: ```
379: 
380: Goldens cover halfway values, adjacent floats, negative zero, non-finite policy, every comparator branch, Unicode, missing versus null, permuted insertion order, and deterministic-ID preimages.
381: 
382: ### Determinism Replay
383: 
384: ```bash
385: for seed in 1 2 3 4 5; do
386:   PARITY_SEED="$seed" cargo test -p ranking-core --test determinism --locked
387: done
388: ```
389: 
390: Also rerun in fresh processes so process-randomized hash state and incidental initialization order cannot remain hidden.
391: 
392: ### Forced WASI Fallback
393: 
394: ```bash
395: NAPI_RS_FORCE_WASI=error \
396:   node test/boundary/forced-wasi-fallback.mjs
397: ```
398: 
399: The test must fail if the native addon loads successfully or if the fallback silently uses a different ABI. Compare the forced-WASI output and JavaScript error behavior against the same TypeScript oracle and native golden corpus.
400: 
401: ---
402: 
403: ## 3. RELATED RESOURCES
